// ============================================================================
// transpose-matrix.epher -- the transpose of a 3x2 matrix
//
// The transpose flips a matrix across its main diagonal: (T)[r][c] =
// M[c][r], so a 3x2 matrix becomes 2x3 (any linear algebra text, e.g.
// Strang, "Introduction to Linear Algebra", 5th ed., sec. 1.6). The
// built-in transpose does it; transposing twice returns the original, and
// a symmetric matrix equals its own transpose, both checked as booleans.
// All values are exact.
//
// Run it from a terminal:  epher "epher scripts/algebra/matrices/transpose-matrix.epher"
//
// The knobs to edit are the matrices A and S below.
// ============================================================================

// ---- the knobs ---------------------------------------------------------------
const A = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]       // 3 rows, 2 columns
const S = [[1, 2], [2, 4]]               // symmetric: equals its transpose

// ---- results -----------------------------------------------------------------
print("transpose:", transpose(A))
print("transposing twice returns A:", transpose(transpose(A)) == A)
print("S equals its transpose:", transpose(S) == S)
// ---- expected output ----
// = [[1, 2], [3, 4], [5, 6]]
// = [[1, 2], [2, 4]]
// = transpose: [[1, 3, 5], [2, 4, 6]]
// = transposing twice returns A: true
// = S equals its transpose: true
